# ComHub AI Development Governance

These rules are mandatory for AI-assisted development in this repository.
They complement `AGENTS.md` and apply to code, configuration, database,
deployment, UI, and documentation changes.

## Read Before Changing Code

Before modifying any business code, routing, configuration, database schema,
deployment file, or UI behavior, read:

- `docs/FEATURE_REGISTRY.md`
- `docs/PROJECT_AUDIT.md`
- `docs/REFACTOR_PLAN.md`

Use these documents to identify the feature owner, current status, dependencies,
risk level, missing tests, and recommended refactor sequence.

## New Features

When adding a new feature, update:

- `docs/FEATURE_REGISTRY.md`
- `docs/CHANGELOG_INTERNAL.md`

If `docs/CHANGELOG_INTERNAL.md` does not exist, create it in the same change.

The registry entry must include status, explanation, frontend entry, core
components, API/server actions, database dependencies, config keys, environment
variables, external services, related files, maintenance risk, refactor
recommendation, test gap, and notes.

## Existing Feature Changes

When modifying an existing feature, document the impact scope:

- Pages
- Components
- API / Server Actions
- Database
- Configuration keys
- Environment variables
- Docker deployment

If an item is not affected, state that explicitly in the implementation notes or
final response.

## Feature Deletion

Do not delete a feature directly.

First:

- Mark the feature as `deprecated` in `docs/FEATURE_REGISTRY.md`.
- Search all references.
- Check page routes, component imports, API routers, server actions, database
  tables, migrations, config keys, environment variables, Docker files, and
  deployment scripts.
- Confirm there are no active dependencies.

Only delete code after the above is complete, and keep the deletion small and
reversible.

## Refactor Rules

- No unplanned large-scale refactors.
- Follow `docs/REFACTOR_PLAN.md`.
- Split refactors into small reversible steps.
- Core flows require tests before refactoring.
- Prefer adapters, formatters, compatibility wrappers, or feature flags before
  removing old behavior.

Core flows include admin settings, runtime config, AI providers/models, pricing,
plans, credits, billing, usage, referral, branding, loading/favicons, default
agents, desktop update, deployment, notifications, memory, Composio, community,
MCP, auth, user defaults, and permission-sensitive admin actions.

## AI Configuration

Never hardcode AI credentials or model routing values.

Do not hardcode:

- AI API keys
- AI base URLs
- AI model names

AI runtime configuration must come from:

- `AI_API_KEY`
- `AI_BASE_URL`
- `AI_MODEL_NAME`

Provider-specific compatibility variables may only be used through the approved
settings/config layer and must be documented in `docs/FEATURE_REGISTRY.md`.

## Docker Deployment

- Docker deployment must use Bind Mounts.
- Do not introduce Docker Named Volumes.
- Deployment changes must describe host paths, container paths, data impact,
  migration needs, and rollback steps.

## Completion Checklist

Before claiming completion, verify:

- Only intended files changed.
- Added features updated `docs/FEATURE_REGISTRY.md`.
- Added features updated `docs/CHANGELOG_INTERNAL.md`.
- Existing feature changes document page/component/API/database/config/env/Docker
  impact.
- Deleted features were marked deprecated and reference-checked first.
- No AI key, base URL, or model name was hardcoded.
- Docker changes use Bind Mounts only.
- Core-flow refactors have tests or documented smoke checks.
- The change is reversible.
